### [Insight Discovery]
You correctly identified that the conjugate of the denominator \(20 - \sqrt{399}\) is \(20 + \sqrt{399}\). Multiplying numerator and denominator by this conjugate uses the difference of squares property, which will eliminate the square root term in the denominator — this is exactly what you need to simplify the fraction inside the outer square root.

### [Operation Formulation]
Multiply **both the numerator and denominator** of the fraction inside the square root by the conjugate \(20 + \sqrt{399}\) (this does not change the value of the fraction).

### [Operation Execution]
$$
\sqrt{\frac{400 \cdot (20+\sqrt{399})}{(20-\sqrt{399})(20+\sqrt{399})}}
= \sqrt{\frac{400(20+\sqrt{399})}{20^2 - (\sqrt{399})^2}}
= \sqrt{400\left(20+\sqrt{399}\right)}
$$
